Step 1: Enable the Metrics Server in Minikube

Because Minikube is minimal by default, enable the metrics addon:

minikube addons enable metrics-server

Verify that the metrics server is running:

kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=metrics-server

Wait until it shows 1/1 Running (it may take 20–30 seconds), then verify node metrics can be fetched:

kubectl top nodes

Step 2: Deploy an App with CPU Requests

Deploy a PHP Apache web server configured with explicit CPU requests:

cat <<EOF > php-apache.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: php-apache
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      run: php-apache
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        run: php-apache
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: php-apache
        image: registry.k8s.io/hpa-example
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "200m"
          limits:
            cpu: "500m"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: php-apache
spec:
  ports:
  - port: 80
  selector:
    run: php-apache
EOF

kubectl apply -f php-apache.yaml



Step 3: Create the HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA)
Define an autoscale policy: keep average CPU utilization at 50% of the requested value (100m),
scaling between a minimum of 1 and maximum of 5 replicas:

kubectl autoscale deployment php-apache --cpu-percent=50 --min=1 --max=5

Inspect the HPA:
kubectl get hpa php-apache

(Under TARGETS, you will initially see <unknown>/50% for a few moments while the Metrics Server
 gathers its first cycle of data, before switching to 0%/50%.)


Step 4: Generate Load and Watch Auto-Scaling
Open a separate terminal window in Codespaces (or run it as a background job) to hammer the 
web service with an infinite HTTP request loop:

kubectl run -i --tty load-generator --rm --image=busybox:1.28 --restart=Never -- /bin/sh -c "while true; do wget -q -O- http://php-apache; done"

In your original terminal, watch the HPA and Pod count scale out in real time:

kubectl get hpa php-apache -w

Within 1–2 minutes, you will watch:

TARGETS spike from 0%/50% to well over 100%/50%.

REPLICAS automatically climb from 1 to 2, 3, 4, or 5.

If you run kubectl get pods -l run=php-apache, you will see new Pods running to share the incoming compute load.

Execute Steps 1 through 3, and confirm once kubectl top nodes reports CPU/memory stats.


